body:
It seems like psalm ignores the result of variable assignments in a branch if there's an InvalidArgument in that branch, even if that argument's type declaration was from phpdoc instead of a real type. This is unintuitive behavior, especially if the InvalidArgument is suppressed.

Observed:

```
ERROR: InvalidArgument - src/experiment.php:11:40 - Argument 1 of my_entity::update expects array<mixed, mixed>|null, string provided                                                                                                          
            $success = $entity->update('');                


ERROR: InvalidArgument - src/experiment.php:13:40 - Argument 1 of my_entity::update expects array<mixed, mixed>|null, string provided                                                                                                          
            $success = $entity->update('');                


ERROR: RedundantCondition - src/experiment.php:16:13 - Found a redundant condition when evaluating $success            
        if ($success) {             
```

Expected: Should emit InvalidArgument but not RedundantCondition.

```
<?php
class my_entity_updater {
    /**
     * @return boolean - true if the update was successful, false otherwise
     */
    public static function update_entity() {
        $success = false;  // If I remove this, then psalm emits UndefinedVariable instead of RedundantCondition.
        $entity = new my_entity();
        // Strangely, removing the rand() % 2 and if/else causes psalm to get a value for $success
        if (rand() % 2 > 0) {
            $success = $entity->update('');
        } else {
            $success = $entity->update('');
        }

        if ($success) {
            echo "Found\n";
        }
        return $success;
    }
}
class my_entity {
    public function __construct() {
    }

    /**
     * @param array|null $setting (This phpdoc is invalid, but **phpdoc** shouldn't affect the inferred return type the caller gets.)
     *
     * @return boolean
     */
    public function update($setting) {
        return rand() % 2 > 0;
    }
}
```
